WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.940
YouTube is rolling out an update to include co-views,

00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:05.860
the estimated number of people watching together on TV

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:07.860
in the public view counts for videos.

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:10.540
YouTube is doing this because viewers are now watching

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:13.800
over 1 billion hours of YouTube on TVs every day

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.940
and the inclusion of co-views in the public view count

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:18.960
will credit creators true reach

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:20.680
with a platform-backed metric

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:23.380
to better inform everything from channel strategy

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:26.520
to brand pitches and provide a more complete picture

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:29.120
of how audiences are engaging with content,

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.500
aligning with TV industry standards,

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.500
including how traditional broadcast and streaming networks

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.640
estimate the total number of people exposed to content.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.380
Co-views apply across formats,

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:42.860
long form, shorts, and live watched on TV.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.200
They don't apply to mobile or desktop.

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:47.520
Co-views won't be applied retroactively.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.820
Historical view counts won't change,

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:53.240
but co-views will apply to new TV views going forward,

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:54.880
including on old videos.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.260
To help creators understand how co-views

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:59.200
are contributing to the total view count,

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:01.960
YouTube Studio is also adding a new metric,

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:05.000
co-view rate, available in the device type breakdown.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:08.320
Just divide the total views, which now include co-views,

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:11.700
by the co-view rate to get views without co-views.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.080
And because co-views only apply to TV,

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:17.560
mobile and desktop will always show a co-view rate of one.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.060
With co-views becoming part of the public view count,

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:22.940
the previous private views co-viewed metric

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:26.440
in YouTube Studio will be sunset in May, 2027.

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:29.320
If you want to export that data for historical analytics,

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:30.980
you can do it up until May.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:33.760
And if you're curious how co-views are estimated,

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:36.980
YouTube doesn't use devices, cameras, microphones,

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:38.560
or anything like that.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:42.460
Co-views are estimated using a privacy safe statistical model

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:44.760
developed by Google research scientists

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:47.620
based on factors like demographic patterns

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:51.060
and viewing times calibrated against ground truth data sources

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:52.360
like industry panels.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:56.000
Co-views don't impact ad revenue or YPP thresholds.

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:57.860
Creator earnings, AdSense payouts,

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.180
and YouTube partner program eligibility

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:03.620
remain based on qualified views and watch hours.

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:06.520
And while co-views also don't impact metrics

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:08.780
like thumbnail impressions, click-through rates,

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:10.020
and average view duration,

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:12.180
watch time will be consistent

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:14.060
with the updated public view counts

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:16.940
to reflect the co-view audience watching together.

